=begin pod :kind("Type") :subkind("class") :category("basic")

=TITLE class RakuAST::Type::Coercion

=SUBTITLE Represent a Raku type that coerces

    class RakuAST::Type::Coercion { }

The C<RakuAST::Type::Coercion> class represents a Raku type that
coerces.

=head2 Object introspection

C<RakuAST::Type::Coercion> object is typically created when parsing
Raku Programming Language code.  So most developers will only need to
know how to introspect the objects created.

=head3 is-coercive

=begin code :preamble<my $type>
say $type.is-coercive;  # True
=end code

The C<is-coercive> method always returns C<True>.

=head3 name

=begin code :preamble<my $type>
say $type.name;  # RakuAST::Name.from-identifier("foo")
=end code

Returns the C<L<RakuAST::Name>|/type/RakuAST/Name> object of this
C<RakuAST::Type::Coercion> object.

=head2 Object creation

There is only one way to create a C<RakuAST::Type::Coercion> object.

=head3 new

=begin code
my $type = RakuAST::Type::Coercion.new(
  base-type  => RakuAST::Type::Simple.from-identifier("Int"),
  constraint => RakuAST::Type::Simple.from-identifier("Str")
);
dd $type;  # Int(Str)
=end code

The C<.new> method creates a C<RakuAST::Type::Coercion> object. It
expects the following named arguments:

=item :base-type - RakuAST::Type object for the base type (required)
=item :constraint - RakuAST::Type object (optional, default: Any)

=end pod
